{"objective":"Scope the architecture-hardening backlog produced by the 2026-11 full-codebase review of eka-cli, eka-core, eka-standard, and eka-mcp, executed in four staged waves.","scope":"In scope: (1) correctness defects that block CI or silently produce unusable knowledge — the unbounded git walk-up breaking five bootstrap tests, and the capture gateway emitting drafts that can never publish. (2) Truthfulness of documented contracts — exchange export atomicity, version surface drift, embedded-standard parity. (3) AI-era surface gaps — a self-describing command surface, MCP confirmation-gate parity, freshness semantics in the CKO envelope. (4) Durability and maintainability — codegraph cache effectiveness, test coverage on the highest-risk packages, and decomposition of the oversized tool dispatcher. Each wave is a separate container so work lands incrementally and reviewable.","outOfScope":"Out of scope (deliberately deferred): migration of the MCP server off protocol 2024-11-05 (a recorded decision to stay, see spk:mcp-protocol-survey — revisit only when a client requires newer semantics); addition of prompts/* and resources/subscribe MCP capabilities (unrequested by current clients); rewriting legacy Markdown draft support in relate (explicitly refused by documented decision); large-scale restructuring of eka-core package boundaries; any change to the EKA Standard conformance rule set beyond correcting the version prose; performance work on the canonical store (SQLite WAL is adequate at current volumes)."}